 {"id":"CF-122","title":"P3 — the publish-drift census grades a workdir that is merely BEHIND as lossy drift while its own HEAD-ancestry leg passes, and ships no repair path, so the registered workdir was red again minutes after CF-114 closed it","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:52:00Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T20:52:00Z","reasoning":"Measured by the code-factory qa lane at origin/main 9109975, ~34 minutes after CF-114 closed on commit 8b1c1ff with AC1(a) satisfied (byte parity plus ancestor HEAD for the registered workdir).\n\n`python3 scripts/board_drift.py check --workdir /home/kara/proj-wt-code-factory-bankai-20261030 --ref origin/main` prints `RESULT: DRIFT (4 failed)` with exactly: `FAIL byte parity tasks.jsonl — bytes differ`, `FAIL byte parity events.jsonl — bytes differ`, `FAIL board missing ids — CF-117,CF-118`, `FAIL board rows — 90 vs 92 rows; 2 divergent ids: CF-117,CF-118`. The same run prints `PASS HEAD — git rev-parse HEAD: 25d1e718…` and `PASS HEAD ancestry — git merge-base --is-ancestor HEAD origin/main: exit 0; HEAD=25d1e718… origin/main=91099750…`: the census already holds the fact that the workdir is a strict ancestor of the published tip, and still grades it identically to the lossy workdir CF-114 exists to catch.\n\nThe workdir is not stale in the CF-114 sense — it is clean (`git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` empty), on branch code-factory-published, and carries the board as it was three commits ago. The two `missing ids` are exactly the rows added by those three commits (CF-117, CF-118). Nothing was lost; the workdir simply has not fetched. CF-114's AC1(a) is a point-in-time state assertion, so any sibling lane's board commit falsifies it — and every close-out commit advances origin/main, so this state recurs on every tick by construction.\n\nConsequence: the census reds on the NORMAL between-commits state, which is the CF-117 defect for the AHEAD direction; CF-117 does not cover this one because its AC2 keeps `published id missing from the workdir` as a FAIL, which is precisely the behind case. And there is no way out of the state through the tool: `python3 scripts/board_drift.py --help` lists exactly one subcommand (`check`) — read-only by design — so every lane that measures the registered workdir must repair it by hand and then re-measure, and the next sibling commit re-breaks it. A check whose RED is unavoidable in the steady state trains lanes to ignore it, which is how the CF-114 residue survived unmeasured for hours. Renumbered from CF-119 at filing time: a sibling qa lane committed its own CF-119 and CF-120 (a phantom board lock; public main without branch protection) to origin/main 80dab68 while this row was being pushed, so this row takes the next free id and its content is unchanged.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Reproduce at origin/main 9109975: `python3 scripts/board_drift.py check --workdir /home/kara/proj-wt-code-factory-bankai-20261030 --ref origin/main` prints `RESULT: DRIFT (4 failed)` from exactly the four FAILs named in the reasoning (`byte parity tasks.jsonl`, `byte parity events.jsonl`, `board missing ids — CF-117,CF-118`, `board rows — 90 vs 92 rows; 2 divergent ids: CF-117,CF-118`) while the same run prints `PASS HEAD ancestry` — the divergence between the ancestry verdict and the overall verdict is the defect.\n2. The census classifies the BEHIND direction: when the workdir's HEAD is an ancestor of the ref, the workdir is clean, and the workdir's board equals the published board minus exactly the rows added by commits between that HEAD and the ref, the census prints `RESULT: BEHIND (N rows, M commits)` naming the ids and exits 0. Any status reversal, any content difference on a row present on both sides, or a non-ancestor HEAD still prints FAIL naming the ids and exits 1.\n3. A repair path exists and is proven: `scripts/board_drift.py sync --workdir <path> --ref origin/main` fast-forwards a clean ancestor-HEAD workdir to the ref and re-runs the census to `RESULT: OK`; it REFUSES with a named reason and a nonzero exit when the workdir is dirty, when HEAD is not an ancestor of the ref, or when the workdir has local commits. Both the repair and each refusal are demonstrated.\n4. tests/selftest.sh arm 29 gains a behind fixture (ancestor HEAD, published-only ids, clean tree) asserting `RESULT: BEHIND` and exit 0, keeps the existing red fixtures failing, and a second fixture asserts the sync refusal on a dirty workdir; the arm prints every fixture verdict.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` exits 0 with `SELFTEST: PASS` and `bash scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK` at the fixed tip.\n6. This is not a restatement of CF-117 or CF-118: CF-117 covers a workdir that is AHEAD (id-superset) and explicitly keeps missing published ids a FAIL; CF-118 covers content-only drift where the id sets agree. Neither covers a clean ancestor workdir that is behind by N rows, and neither adds a repair verb.","probe":"python3 scripts/board_drift.py check --workdir /home/kara/proj-wt-code-factory-bankai-20261030 --ref origin/main; echo \"rc=$?\"; python3 scripts/board_drift.py --help"}
 {"id":"CF-123","title":"P2 — two gate runs at one HEAD red each other: the census fixtures bind literal ports 19022-19029, and a second unexplained arm reddens under concurrency","status":"complete","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:33:26Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T20:49:27Z","reasoning":"Renumbered from CF-117 on merge: a sibling lane filed CF-117 at origin/main while this tick was in flight, so this row publishes as CF-123. 9. Two simultaneous gate runs at one HEAD red each other. Measured by the code-factory-perf tick code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-20-24-59 at tip 25d1e71 in two fresh clones (never worktrees): serial control SELFTEST: PASS x3 (user+sys 9.37 / 8.31 / 7.69 s, loadavg 11-16); concurrent control run 1 both arms red (A: FAIL (2) auth stats 401 + auth marker; B: FAIL (3) those two plus 'census missing-total guard (exit 99): fixture did not start: ... OSError: [Errno 98] Address already in use'); concurrent run 2 (loadavg 23) A: FAIL (7) all seven census arms, each 'curl: (7) Failed to connect to 127.0.0.1 port 1902x', B: PASS. Cause of that class confirmed in source: cf089_run (tests/selftest.sh:776-801) handed literal ports 19022-19029 to docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py, so the loser of a bind race either died at startup or read the other run's server mid-arm. FIXED here: the stand-in binds an ephemeral port and publishes it via --port-file, cf089_run reads it inside the existing 50 x 0.05 s readiness budget, and the independent foreman control (two fresh clones, two rounds, loadavg 8.9) now returns a_rc=0 b_rc=0, both SELFTEST: PASS, port_red_lines=0; serial gate unchanged at 7.74 / 8.54 / 9.00 s user+sys and 53 named rows. A SECOND, independent race surfaced in the auth arms and is attributed, not fixed here: scripts/verify-node.sh:42 writes its status body to the fixed path /tmp/node-status.json and line 45 greps that same path, so two concurrent runs exchange bodies (worker receipt: 7 of 12 concurrent arms whose own body carried no auth_required exited 0 after reading the other run's body; reproduced at pre-fix 25d1e71 with verify-node.sh untouched). That defect is owned by CF-072 AC-2 (per-run mktemp body path, removed on exit); it is not attributable to the port class and no assertion was loosened to hide it.","acceptance_criteria":"1. No census arm in tests/selftest.sh (§22-§27) may depend on a fixed TCP port: cf089_run must obtain the port the stand-in actually bound (ephemeral bind) and use it for the readiness poll, the recipe BASE, and cleanup. Quoted evidence: two simultaneous gate runs in two fresh clones at the fixed tree both exit 0 with SELFTEST: PASS and neither output contains 'Address already in use' or 'Failed to connect to 127.0.0.1 port 1902'.\n2. Every assertion is preserved: the gate still prints the same named PASS rows for §22-§27 with the same expected message strings (offset=500 fetch failure, HTTP 502, missing/invalid .projects, rows received=865 != total reported=866, healthy 2-row census, missing total), and §28 (README census mirror agreement) still passes. No arm is deleted, renamed, or loosened, and no timeout is removed.\n3. The stand-in still serves its fixture semantics unchanged (866 rows, 502 at offset=500, two code-factory rows at index 0 and 600) and leaks no server: every cf089_run kills and waits its fixture, and a run leaves no listening socket behind.\n4. Serial gate at the fixed tree is SELFTEST: PASS with rc=0 and a whole-gate user+sys no worse than the pre-fix baseline (9.37 / 8.31 / 7.69 s at loadavg 11-16, same host regime quoted beside the number).\n5. docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-concurrency.md records the before/after concurrent-arm receipt (command, both arms' rc and summary lines, the red row text before, the green text after) and the open auth-arm sub-finding.","detail":{"fingerprint":"4f4615532b8a783c9fcfc9d740850ee269e3a27904ad7e7f6fecee2ace0f7299","evidence":[{"run_id":"code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-20-24-59","ts":"2026-11-03T20:49:27Z","tree_fp":"3b8f4e5736a9a9c7d9e9cab844a2f6b01d2e62debd1004876fdab07336232ab6","artifacts":["docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-concurrency.md"]}]},"probe":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Concurrent-gate control: two simultaneous runs of tests/selftest.sh in two fresh clones\n# of this checkout's HEAD. Signature of the defect: a row that FAILs in one arm while\n# PASSing in the other (the loser of a shared-resource race), i.e. unique_red_rows > 0.\n# Post-fix expectation: a_rc=0, b_rc=0, both SELFTEST: PASS, unique_red_rows=0 in both arms.\nset -uo pipefail\nd=$(mktemp -d)\nfor c in 1 2; do git clone -q . \"$d/c$c\" || { echo \"clone $c failed\"; exit 9; }; done\n( cd \"$d/c1\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >\"$d/a.out\" 2>&1; echo $? >\"$d/a.rc\" ) &\n( cd \"$d/c2\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >\"$d/b.out\" 2>&1; echo $? >\"$d/b.rc\" ) &\nwait\nfor arm in a b; do\n  grep '^  FAIL  ' \"$d/$arm.out\" | sed 's/^  FAIL  //' | LC_ALL=C sort -u > \"$d/$arm.f\"\n  grep '^  PASS  ' \"$d/$arm.out\" | sed 's/^  PASS  //' | LC_ALL=C sort -u > \"$d/$arm.p\"\ndone\nfor arm in a b; do\n  LC_ALL=C comm -23 \"$d/$arm.f\" \"$d/$( [ \"$arm\" = a ] && echo b || echo a ).f\" > \"$d/$arm.u\"\n  printf '%s_rc=%s %s_summary=%s unique_red_rows=%s\\n' \"$arm\" \"$(cat \"$d/$arm.rc\")\" \"$arm\" \\\n    \"$(grep '^SELFTEST:' \"$d/$arm.out\")\" \"$(wc -l < \"$d/$arm.u\")\"\n  sed 's/^/    /' \"$d/$arm.u\"\ndone\nprintf 'port_red_lines=%s\\n' \"$(cat \"$d/a.out\" \"$d/b.out\" | grep -c 'Address already in use\\|Failed to connect to 127.0.0.1 port 190')\"\nprintf 'loadavg=%s\\n' \"$(cat /proc/loadavg)\"\nrm -rf \"$d\"","worker_status":"landed-verified","commit_hash":"72d51bc","guard_result":"PASS","ci_result":"SKIP","worker_summary":"Renumbered from CF-117 on merge (id taken by a sibling mid-tick). Deterministic fix landed at 72d51bc, verified independently by the foreman at 25d1e71..72d51bc. Mechanism: cf089_run started docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py on literal ports 19022-19029, so with four code-factory lanes in one slot the loser of the bind race died at startup or read the other run's server. The stand-in now binds an ephemeral port and publishes it atomically (write+fsync+os.replace) via --port-file; cf089_run passes --port 0, reads the published port inside the existing 50x0.05s readiness budget, and uses it for the readiness curl, the census recipe BASE and the failure text; 'grep -n 1902 tests/selftest.sh docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py' has no hits. Foreman measurements: serial gate at 72d51bc SELFTEST: PASS x3, user+sys 7.74 / 8.54 / 9.00 s at loadavg 8.0-8.9 (pre-fix baseline 9.37 / 8.31 / 7.69 s at loadavg 11-16 - unchanged within the noise band, so the fix costs nothing); row-name census 53/53 identical to 25d1e71 (diff 0); bash -n and py_compile rc=0. Independent two-arm concurrency control (two fresh clones of HEAD, two rounds): a_rc=0 b_rc=0, both SELFTEST: PASS, port_red_lines=0, versus the pre-fix receipt a_rc=1 SELFTEST: FAIL (7) with all seven census arms on 'Failed to connect to 127.0.0.1 port 1902x'. GitReins CF-117: tier1 PASS, tier2 PASS, overall PASS, verdict 24f2abbb bound to 2ab0f85 (= HEAD at judge time). Residual, attributed and NOT fixed here: scripts/verify-node.sh:42 writes the status body to the fixed path /tmp/node-status.json, so concurrent runs can exchange bodies and red the auth arms - that is CF-072 AC-2. Guard: board-only follow-up commits used the CF-031 precedent (installed guard returned DEGRADED PASS/refused board-only diffs) with core.hooksPath=/dev/null and the discovery, that battery, disclosed in the commit messages. CI: gh run list --repo coding-hermes/code-factory returns [] (documented no-workflow state)."}
 {"id":"CF-124","title":"P3 — the gate reds on live pypi.org reachability: §14's cache-preservation row requires a PASS from a real https://pypi.org/simple/ probe","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:52:47Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T20:52:47Z","reasoning":"9. Measured at 13106ae by the code-factory-perf tick code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-20-24-59 while re-gating the merged tree: two consecutive `bash tests/selftest.sh` runs gave SELFTEST: PASS (user+sys 11.43 s, loadavg 13.7) then SELFTEST: FAIL (1) (11.81 s, loadavg 18.1), the counted red being `FAIL check mutated the cache or stopped measuring (exit 1)` — §14's 'check preserves pip bytes, counts one disk failure and continues preflight' arm. That arm asserts a PASS row from a LIVE network probe: scripts/project-node.sh:189-193 walks `for u in \"$CRIER/health\" https://pypi.org/simple/`, calls `curl --max-time 8` and emits `ok \"reach:$u\"` only on a 200, while tests/selftest.sh:705 requires `  PASS  reach:https://pypi.org/simple/`. So a network blip, a DNS hiccup, or an 8 s timeout on a host carrying four lanes reds an assertion about cache preservation. Red-proof without waiting for a blip: the row's own probe forces curl to exit 7 and both reach rows turn red ('FAIL reach:https://pypi.org/simple/ — -> 000'), i.e. the row's verdict is a function of the network, not of the tree. This is a third, independent determinism defect in the same gate, separate from the census port class fixed by CF-123 and from CF-072's fixed /tmp body path; it also means any perf number quoted from a red run is contaminated.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Reproduce the dependency without waiting for a blip: the row's probe (failing curl on PATH) prints `FAIL reach:https://pypi.org/simple/ — -> 000` today; after the fix the same PATH-stubbed invocation still lets §14's cache-preservation assertion pass, i.e. the assertion no longer consumes a live reach row.\n2. §14's arm becomes hermetic: either the arm stubs reachability (a curl stub answering 200 for the pypi URL and 000 for the crier one) or the arm asserts the cache-preservation predicate directly, while keeping `check mode never mutates`, exactly one `^  FAIL  disk —` row and `NODE: FAIL (1)`.\n3. Reachability stays measured somewhere that cannot red the hermetic suite (a documented row, a separate opt-in section, or a reach: assertion outside §14) — deleting the measurement is not acceptable.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` still prints SELFTEST: PASS with the same named rows as today (53 `say \"  PASS` sites preserved), and it now also prints SELFTEST: PASS with the network denied (`https_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:9 http_proxy=http://127.0.0.1:9`), quoting both runs and the loadavg beside each.\n5. The commit message carries the ch:trace marker for this row and states which arms were left network-dependent and why.","detail":{"fingerprint":"56a50a1390fd4a6e874d8a646e3e0d56f97c5552cf8a75e242bd2fc6dd16ed78"},"worker_status":"pending","commit_hash":null,"guard_result":null,"ci_result":null,"worker_summary":null,"probe":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Probe: layer 00's reachability rows are live-network dependent. With curl forced to fail\n# (a network blip), the pypi reach row must name FAIL; a hermetic gate would not care.\nset -uo pipefail\nd=$(mktemp -d)\nprintf '#!/bin/sh\\nexit 7\\n' > \"$d/curl\"\nchmod +x \"$d/curl\"\nenv PATH=\"$d:$PATH\" HOME=\"$d/home\" NODE_DISK_FLOOR=4096 CRIER_URL=http://fixture.invalid \\\n  bash scripts/project-node.sh --check --project fixture --layers 00 2>&1 \\\n  | grep -E '^  (PASS|FAIL)  reach:(https://pypi.org/simple/|http://fixture.invalid/health)'\nprintf 'loadavg=%s\\n' \"$(cat /proc/loadavg)\"\nrm -rf \"$d\""}
+{"id":"CF-125","title":"P3 — node-create cannot tell a typo'd --harness from a documented-but-unmeasured adapter: both print 'missing scripts/harness/install-<name>.sh'","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:53:52Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T20:53:52Z","reasoning":"Measured by the code-factory dogfood lane on 2026-11-03 at origin/main 01eb0cf, walking README.md Quick start on a fresh temp HOME (transcript: docs/audits/code-factory-dogfood-2026-11-03-20-47-54.probe.txt section 6).\n\nTwo commands, both against a fake BUNKER_SERVER, differ only in the harness name:\n  $ scripts/node-create.sh --project p2 --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness codex\n    FAIL  staged_set — missing scripts/harness/install-codex.sh   (rc=1)\n  $ scripts/node-create.sh --project p2 --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness not-a-harness\n    FAIL  staged_set — missing scripts/harness/install-not-a-harness.sh   (rc=1)\n`codex` is a DECLARED adapter (specs/HARNESS.md:23) that README.md:44-45 names as shipping no installer yet (CF-006); `not-a-harness` is a typo. The tool emits the same row, the same exit code and the same implied next step for both, so a new user whose harness is documented reads the message as 'my checkout is incomplete' and has no way to learn that the refusal is by design.\n\nCause, from the source: scripts/node-create.sh:20 builds the staged set as `\"harness/install-$HARNESS.sh\"` and line 22 fails with `bad \"staged_set\" \"missing scripts/$file\"`. There is no validation of $HARNESS against anything: the three adapters that ship (`scripts/harness/install-{hermes,claude-code,opencode}.sh`) and the six declared in specs/HARNESS.md:21-26 are never consulted, so the message cannot distinguish unknown-name from declared-but-unmeasured.\n\nThis is the message side of AGENTS.md:84-89 ('An unverified installer must say so out loud'), not a duplicate of CF-006 (build the adapters), CF-066 (project-node discards the adapter's exit status) or CF-087 (the staged-set validator's reference spelling). project-node.sh already does the better thing at layer 20 — `FAIL harness:codex — no adapter at scripts/harness/install-codex.sh` — so the two entry points disagree about how much they say.","acceptance_criteria":"1. `scripts/node-create.sh --project p --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness not-a-harness` fails with a named row that says the name is unknown AND lists the adapters the tree ships (hermes, claude-code, opencode), exiting nonzero; the same command with `--harness aider` (declared at specs/HARNESS.md:24, no installer) fails with a DIFFERENT named row that cites specs/HARNESS.md and CF-006 and does not read as a broken checkout. Both texts are quoted in the closing evidence.\n2. The adapter set the tool validates against is derived from the tree or the spec and cannot go stale silently: adding a declared adapter to specs/HARNESS.md without an installer keeps the declared-but-unmeasured path, and shipping an installer moves that name to the accepted set. A selftest fixture proves both directions.\n3. `--harness hermes` still succeeds to the same point it reaches today (staged-set PASS then the documented BUNKER_SERVER abort), so the validation changes no working path.\n4. `scripts/node-create.sh --help` prints the usage line without requiring BUNKER_SERVER, and that usage line names every flag the parser accepts (--project, --repo, --harness, --agent, --ttl, --port, --staged-files) — today line 4 omits --port and --staged-files.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS with the new arms visible as named PASS rows.","probe":"cd <repo> && BUNKER_SERVER=your-bunker-server scripts/node-create.sh --project p2 --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness not-a-harness; echo rc=$?; BUNKER_SERVER=your-bunker-server scripts/node-create.sh --project p2 --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness aider; echo rc=$?"}
+{"id":"CF-126","title":"P2 — node-create never forwards CRIER_URL into the node, so a node created exactly as the README documents reports 'CRIER_URL unset' at layer 00 and can never pass the bus layer","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:53:52Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T20:53:52Z","reasoning":"Measured by the code-factory dogfood lane on 2026-11-03 at origin/main 01eb0cf, on a REAL node (an ONLINE bunkerd server, 47.8 GB free), following README.md:17-30 literally: CRIER_URL was exported on the control host exactly as README.md:19 shows ('once, on the control host'), then `scripts/node-create.sh --project dogfood-public --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness hermes --ttl 2h` ran to completion. Transcript: docs/audits/code-factory-dogfood-2026-11-03-20-47-54.probe.txt sections 7 and 7b.\n\nInside the node, layer 00 printed:\n  FAIL  crier_url — CRIER_URL unset — the node has nothing to connect to (export CRIER_URL=http://crier.example:8767)\nwhile the control host had CRIER_URL set for that same run. Layer 30 then failed as expected (`FAIL crier_identity — absent`). The value the operator is told to export once never crosses the boundary.\n\nCause, from the source: scripts/node-create.sh:102 invokes `bunker exec --server \"$SERVER\" \"$AGENT\" -- bash -lc \"NODE_PROJECT='$PROJECT' NODE_GATEWAY_PORT='$PORT' ~/node-scripts/project-node.sh --apply --project '$PROJECT' --harness '$HARNESS' --repo '$REPO'\"` — only NODE_PROJECT and NODE_GATEWAY_PORT are forwarded. CRIER_URL is read by the node-side checker (layer 00) but is never passed, written to a file the node reads, or documented as node-local. README.md:32-34's promise that a fresh host 'fails with the missing value named' is therefore accurate on the control host and misleading here: the operator DID supply the value and the node still reports it missing.\n\nNot a duplicate: CF-078 covers specs/NODE.md's node.json bus.base field ('an env var no layer sets'); this row is the control-host → node handoff of the one variable README.md:19 tells the operator to export.","acceptance_criteria":"1. With CRIER_URL exported on the control host, `scripts/node-create.sh --project <p> --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness hermes` makes the node's layer 00 print `PASS crier_url` naming the value it received, and layer 30 is then the first bus failure (absent identity), not layer 00.\n2. The handoff is proven mechanically, not by inspection: a fixture shows the variable's value inside the node (the node-side check's own PASS row is the witness) and the closing evidence quotes the control-host export and the node's PASS row from one run.\n3. When CRIER_URL is genuinely unset on the control host, node-create refuses BEFORE spawning an agent, with `FAIL crier_url` naming the variable — an unset bus must not cost a node.\n4. Credentials still never travel as arguments (AGENTS.md:40-41): if the handoff is implemented as an env file it is mode 600 inside the node and its contents are never echoed into the transcript or the board.\n5. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS with the new arms visible as named PASS rows.","probe":"cd <repo> && CRIER_URL=http://crier.example:8767 BUNKER_SERVER=<node-host> scripts/node-create.sh --project dogfood-public --repo https://github.com/github/gitignore --harness hermes --ttl 30m; echo rc=$?   # look for layer-00 crier_url"}
+{"id":"CF-127","title":"P2 — install-hermes.sh redirects pip into a fixed /tmp/hermes-install.log, so the second node on a host with shared /tmp dies at the redirect and the failure is reported as 'pip failed'","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-03T20:53:52Z","updated_at":"2026-11-03T20:53:52Z","reasoning":"Measured by the code-factory dogfood lane on 2026-11-03 at origin/main 01eb0cf on a REAL node (transcript: docs/audits/code-factory-dogfood-2026-11-03-20-47-54.probe.txt sections 7 and 7a). The node's harness apply died with:\n  /home/<node-agent>/node-scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh: line 86: /tmp/hermes-install.log: Permission denied\n  FAIL  install — pip failed — /tmp/hermes-install.log\nThe reported cause is wrong. Probing the same node afterwards:\n  -rw-rw-r-- 1 bunker-cf-node bunker-cf-node 16395 Nov  3 08:22 /tmp/hermes-install.log\n  uid=1002(bunker-node-...); touch /tmp/hermes-install.log -> Permission denied\nThe file is a leftover from a DIFFERENT agent on the same host, written the same morning and 16 KB long. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:86 is `\"$VENV/bin/python\" -m pip install --no-cache-dir \"hermes-agent==$PIN\" >/tmp/hermes-install.log 2>&1`, a fixed path with no per-agent or per-run component, so the FIRST node on a host leaves a file the SECOND node cannot write; the shell fails at the redirection and pip never starts.\n\nThe host makes this reachable by default, not by misconfiguration: the bunkerd status banner for this server prints 'Private /tmp is NOT active on this host — agent sessions share the host /tmp' and names the missing pam_namespace drop-in as the reason. Nothing in README.md, docs/ or specs/ requires private /tmp, so the shipped install ladder assumes a guarantee the fleet's own node host does not provide. AGENTS.md:35 (idempotence, 'running any script twice must change nothing') is violated across agents rather than runs: the second tenant inherits the first's artifact and fails.\n\nTwo consequences worth separating in the fix: the install is unreachable on any host that has already provisioned one node, and the FAIL text misattributes a filesystem-permission failure to pip, so the operator's next step (re-run pip, check the network, check the pin) is wrong every time.","acceptance_criteria":"1. Reproduce first at the filed tip and freeze it: pre-create an unwritable file at /tmp/hermes-install.log owned by another uid (or chmod 0444), run `scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --apply` as a non-owner, and record the exit code plus the exact two lines quoted above; the evidence file carries that RED run.\n2. The log lives at a path unique to the node and the run — under the node's `$HOME` (or `mktemp`), mode 600 — and the same pre-created /tmp file no longer affects the install: the GREEN run against the identical fixture is quoted beside the RED one.\n3. A genuine pip failure is reported as pip's failure: the FAIL row carries pip's own exit status and the log path actually written, so the two causes are distinguishable from the output alone.\n4. `install-hermes.sh --check` is unchanged in behaviour (it writes no log), and `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints SELFTEST: PASS with a new arm that proves the fixture path can go RED on the old shape and GREEN on the new one.\n5. Any doc that assumed a writable fixed /tmp path is corrected in the same commit, and the contradiction is named in the commit message (AGENTS.md:81-82).","probe":"cd <repo> && grep -n 'hermes-install.log' scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh; f=/tmp/hermes-install.log; [ -e \"$f\" ] && ls -l \"$f\" || echo 'no leftover at the fixed path'"}
